.TH CCOL_LOG_ALERT 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_log_alert \- emit a log line at ALERT level with a backtrace
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

ccol_log_alert(clog lg, const char *fmt, ...);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Writes a log line at CLOG_ALERT level.  It captures __FILE__, __LINE__
and __func__ by itself.  After it writes the structured log line, it
appends a stack backtrace in the same way as ccol_log_error():

  ts=... level=ALERT src=file.c:42 func=handle msg="disk full"
  \et#0 ./myapp(handle+0x1a) [0x7f...]
  \et#1 ./myapp(main+0x42) [0x7f...]

ALERT maps to RFC 5424 syslog severity 1, which reads "alert; action
must be taken immediately".  In CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG mode the library writes
each backtrace frame as a separate syslog message.  That message uses
the same PRI, TIMESTAMP and MSGID as the original record.

ALERT sits between ERROR, at severity 3, and FATAL, at severity 0, in
the order of clog_level_t.  Use it for a condition that needs the
attention of an operator at once.  Such a condition need not stop the
process.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
lg     Logger handle (clog).
fmt    printf-style format string.
\&...    Format arguments (optional).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
ccol_log_alert does NOT stop the process, and ccol_log_error does not
either.  Use ccol_log_fatal when the process must stop.
A backtrace needs the binary to be linked with -rdynamic.  The capture
itself can fail.  It fails on a platform with no execinfo.h, and on a
transient allocation failure while the library resolves symbol names.
The logger then writes one "backtrace unavailable" marker line in place
of the usual output for each frame.  In CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG mode it writes
a marker record instead.  The omission is therefore visible, and not
silent.
The backtrace write is protected by the shared write lock, so the
backtrace and the preceding log line always appear contiguously.
Memory pressure can last for a time.  The content of a record can also
leave little room before the internal buffer cap.  A single backtrace
frame that the library cannot format is then omitted.  A shorter frame
that follows it can still go out.  The library never writes a frame
that is incomplete, so every line that it writes is a complete,
well-formed record.  When not one frame fits at all, one "backtrace
unavailable" marker line takes their place.  In CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG mode
that is a marker record.  It is the same marker that the library uses
when the capture itself fails.  In CLOG_FMT_JSON mode the library can
fail to embed the backtrace at all under such pressure.  A
"bt_error":"unavailable" key then takes the place of "bt", so the
omission is visible and not silent.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_log_alert(lg, "primary database unreachable, failing over");
ccol_log_alert(lg, "disk usage at %d%%, writes may fail soon", pct);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_log_error (3),
.BR ccol_log_fatal (3),
.BR clog_open_fd_mp (3)
